# Component Prop Namespacing

When a display controller clones its children (via `React.cloneElement`) to pass
props down, all injected props **must be grouped under a single object key named
after the component** (lowercase).

## Rule

```
cloneElement(child, { <componentName>: { ...props } })
```

## Example — Fold

`FoldDisplay` injects `isOpen` and `toggle` under the `fold` key:

```jsx
// ✅ correct
cloneElement(child, { fold: { isOpen, toggle } })

// ❌ incorrect — props are spread at the top level
cloneElement(child, { isOpen, toggle })
```

Child components destructure from that namespaced key:

```jsx
// ✅ correct
const FoldTrigger = ({ fold: { isOpen, toggle } = {}, children }) => ...
const FoldContent = ({ fold: { isOpen } = {}, children }) => ...
```

This keeps the prop surface of each child unambiguous and prevents collisions
when components are nested or composed together.

See [src/components/Fold/fold.jsx](mdc:src/components/Fold/fold.jsx) for the
reference implementation.
